<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm Excited! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I'm Happy.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Excited!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures. reading_2: Participate in choral reading sessions, supported by the teacher. phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can match pictures of facial expressions (e.g., smiling, frowning) to words like 'happy' or 'sad'. Can recognize simple emotion-related words in choral reading with support from the teacher (e.g., clapping when hearing the word 'happy' and pointing to a picture of a sad face when hearing the word 'sad' during choral reading). Can recognize letters of the alphabet in familiar words related to feelings and emotions (e.g., pointing to the letter 'H' in the word 'happy' and saying 'H').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by singing a "How Are You?" song (e.g., "If you're happy and you know it..."). While singing, the teacher shows large flashcards of different facial expressions (happy, sad, excited, tired, hungry, thirsty, sleepy). Students mimic the expressions and point to the corresponding flashcard as the emotion is sung.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the phrases "I'm happy," "I'm excited," and "I'm sad" using clear gestures and tone of voice, pointing to appropriate flashcards. The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /h/ in 'happy' and /e/ in 'excited' by exaggerating the sounds. For example, "H-H-Happy!" and "E-E-Excited!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using visuals and TPR.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a smiling face) "Am I happy?" (Students nod or say "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a frowning face and points to the word 'happy' below it) "Is this 'happy'?" (Students shake their heads or say "No!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Big book or projected story with large, colorful illustrations and simple sentences. Genre: Simple narrative. Register: Child-friendly. Format: Illustrated text.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher introduces a short story titled "How Are We Feeling Today?" displayed in a big book or projected onto a screen. Each page has a simple sentence and a large picture depicting an emotion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sentences: "I'm happy!" (picture of a smiling child). "I'm sad." (picture of a frowning child). "I'm excited!" (picture of a child jumping). "I'm sleepy." (picture of a child yawning).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads each page slowly and clearly, pointing to the words as they are read and emphasizing the emotion. Students listen and observe the words and pictures.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Responding to questions about feelings: Answering 'How are you?' with simple 'I'm...' statements (e.g., 'I'm excited.'). Discussing feelings: Talking about emotions and recognizing others' feelings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (with TPR/Pointing):</b> After reading, the teacher asks: "Is this story about animals or feelings?" (Students point to their hearts for 'feelings'.) "Does the boy feel happy in the story?" (Teacher shows a happy picture, students give thumbs up).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of facial expressions (happy, sad, excited) and individual word cards ('happy', 'sad', 'excited'). As the teacher points to a picture from the story, students point to the matching word card.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simple sentences (e.g., 'I'm happy.'), vocabulary (happy, sad, tired, hungry, thirsty, calm, sleepy, excited, sick, warm, angry), phonemic awareness (recognizing letters 'H' in 'happy', 'E' in 'excited', and introducing W, X, Y, Z).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word-Picture Match & Choral Reading (Controlled Practice):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes individual worksheets with three columns: pictures of facial expressions (e.g., happy, sad, excited), a word bank, and blank lines. Students draw a line from each picture to the correct word in the word bank.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then displays large emotion word cards (e.g., 'happy', 'sad', 'excited'). Students participate in choral reading, repeating each word after the teacher while pointing to it.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> The teacher highlights specific letters. "Look at the word 'happy'. Can you find the letter 'H'?" (Students point). "H for happy!" (Choral repetition). "Look at 'excited'. Can you find the letter 'E'?" (Students point). "E for excited!" (Choral repetition). The teacher briefly introduces letter cards W, X, Y, Z by showing them and saying their names, without focusing on sounds yet, connecting to a simple alphabet song.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Strip Fill-in (Semi-controlled):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher presents simple sentence strips on the board, such as "I'm _______." with a missing emotion word. The teacher places a picture cue (e.g., a happy face) next to the blank.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students chorally read the full sentence, identifying the correct word from previously introduced vocabulary (e.g., "I'm happy!"). The teacher guides them to read aloud together, ensuring they track the words with their fingers.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Find the Feeling Word" (Pair Check):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards (showing emotions like happy, sad, tired, excited) and corresponding word cards.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A picks an emotion picture card. Student B finds the matching word card and reads it aloud to Student A. They then swap roles. Students help each other if they struggle, pointing to the letters they recognize.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Emotion Sentence Hunt & Match.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher prepares large poster boards, each with a specific emotion picture (e.g., a big happy face, a big sad face, a big excited face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher hides several small sentence strips around the classroom. Each strip contains a simple sentence like "I'm happy.", "I'm sad.", "I'm excited.", "I'm tired." (with a small matching picture on the strip).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students, individually or in small groups, "hunt" for the sentence strips. When a student finds a strip, they must read the sentence aloud (with teacher support if needed) and then take it to the correct emotion poster board to stick it next to the matching picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to say "I found 'I'm happy'!" and match it to the happy poster.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Students are actively reading about and recognizing feelings (I'm Happy, I'm Excited!) in a hands-on, engaging way. The activity encourages both individual reading and collaborative checking.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading fluency for simple emotion sentences, recognition of familiar words, and matching written words to visual cues.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Emotion Sentence Hunt & Match" and a quick individual matching activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a simple worksheet with 3 pictures of facial expressions (happy, sad, excited) and a mixed list of the three words. They draw a line from each picture to its correct matching word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher observes students during the "Emotion Sentence Hunt" activity, noting their ability to find, read, and correctly match the sentence strips to the emotion posters.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can match pictures of facial expressions to their written words ('happy', 'sad', 'excited').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates in choral reading sessions with support from the teacher.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes familiar letters (e.g., 'H' in 'happy', 'E' in 'excited') when prompted.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to read simple emotion sentences, even with minimal support.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up a 'happy' word card) "Can you read this word?" (Students nod or point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you find a word and match it today? Thumbs up if yes!" (Students show thumbs up or down).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new feelings words can you read with your eyes today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How do you feel when you read about different feelings?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the reading activities were most effective in helping Kinder students match spoken words to their written form and participate in choral reading?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did students recognize familiar letters ('H', 'E') within the emotion words? What adjustments are needed for reinforcing letters W, X, Y, Z?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the tasks appropriately challenging for their CEFR A1.2 level, or did some students need more scaffolding for reading?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific vocabulary items (e.g., thirsty, angry) need more reinforcement in the next lesson's activities?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the words they have now learned to read to actively express their own feelings and ask others how they feel, focusing on oral production and simple dialogue.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>